// Test: what a float *is* — equality, ordering against NaN, and how it prints.
//
// The integer range got a rule (11–13) before the float did, and the float had
// more disagreements than the integer, not fewer. Eleven of them, none in the
// corpus, all silent:
//
//   · the tree-walker compared floats with an ABSOLUTE tolerance, so every pair
//     closer than 2.2e-16 was "equal" — including 1e-20 and -5e-20;
//   · the register VM folded NaN's missing ordering into "equal", so NaN was
//     equal to everything, itself included;
//   · the OCaml engine used OCaml's total `compare`, which sorts NaN below every
//     number, so `nan < 1.0` was true;
//   · three engines answered `nan <= 1.0` with true, because a three-state
//     comparator (-1/0/1) cannot say "no ordering" and they all read 0;
//   · the browser engine printed `Infinity`, dropped the sign of -0, and
//     switched to exponent notation past 1e21 and below 1e-6.

<# std/math => m

// ── How a float prints ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Always flat digits, never an exponent: `#^` is the operator that asks for
// scientific notation, and it is tested at the end of this file.
>> 0.1 ¶
>> 0.5 ¶
>>(1.0 / 3.0) ¶
>>(0.1 + 0.2) ¶
>> 1.0e16 ¶
>> 1.0e21 ¶
>> 1.0e-7 ¶
>> 1.5e-10 ¶
>>(0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3) ¶

// Signed zero: -0.0 is not 0.0's spelling, it is a different bit pattern, and
// every engine prints the sign.
>>(-0.0) ¶
>>(0.0 * -1.0) ¶

// ── Equality is exact ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IEEE-754 equality, like the language's other three engines always had. A
// program that wants a tolerance names it.
>>(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3) ¶
>>(1.0e-20 == 2.0e-20) ¶
>>(1.0e-20 == -5.0e-20) ¶
>>(0.0000000000000000001 == 0.0) ¶

// Int/Float promotion still holds — this is what the tolerance was mistakenly
// introduced for, and promotion is what actually delivers it.
>>(1.0 == 1) ¶
>>(1.0 >= 1) ¶
>>(1.0 <= 1) ¶

// ── NaN compares false in every direction ───────────────────────────────────
n = m::sqrt(-1.0)
>> n ¶
>>(n == n) ¶
>>(n <> n) ¶
>>(n == 0.0) ¶
>>(n < 1.0) ¶
>>(n > 1.0) ¶
>>(n <= 1.0) ¶
>>(n >= 1.0) ¶
>>(n <= n) ¶
>>(1.0 <= n) ¶

// ── Infinity is a value ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
i = 1.0e308 * 10.0
>> i ¶
>>(-1.0e308 * 10.0) ¶
>> 1.0e400 ¶
>>(i > 1.0e308) ¶
>>(i == i) ¶
>>(i - i) ¶
>>(i * 0.0) ¶

// ── Scientific notation, when asked for ─────────────────────────────────────
>> #^|1234567.0| ¶
>> #^.2|1234567.0| ¶
